-10-18T15:33:22Z","role":"primary","sha256":"4341c316d2ba2ec1e37f184e8ac1251def4368da78262633f1197174cd671aab","supports":["licenses.code"],"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/billy-enrizky/openbrowser-ai/HEAD/LICENSE"}],"id":"github:billy-enrizky/openbrowser-ai","identifiers":{},"kind":"framework","last_verified_at":"2026-10-18T15:33:16Z","licenses":{"code":{"basis":"repository LICENSE header","declared":"MIT","retrieved_at":"2026-10-18T15:33:22Z","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/billy-enrizky/openbrowser-ai/HEAD/LICENSE"},"data":null,"document":null,"weights":null},"links":[{"rel":"primary","url":"https://github.com/billy-enrizky/openbrowser-ai"}],"name":"OpenBrowser","notes":"Published on PyPI as openbrowser-ai. The CodeAgent (src/openbrowser/code_use/service.py) sends a compact serialized DOM state with element indices and, unless vision is turned off, a JPEG screenshot; the model replies with Python that the executor runs in a persistent namespace. The MCP server exposes a single execute_code tool over the same namespace, and a CLI daemon keeps a browser session alive between commands. The repository also holds a hosted-platform backend and frontend, and infra/training with SFT, online GRPO and diffusion-language-model RL code for FormFactory form filling; the README links the two related papers on ResearchGate. Token and latency comparisons with other browser tools are publisher-reported.","published_at":null,"relations":[],"review_status":"curated","scope":"core","summary":"Python browser-automation framework whose CodeAgent shows a model indexed DOM state and an optional screenshot, then runs the Python it writes against Chrome DevTools Protocol helpers in a persistent namespace.","topics":["web-navigation","automation","tool-integration"],"updated_at":null,"url":"https://github.com/billy-enrizky/openbrowser-ai"}
